WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.738
At a café, the card reader shows an error message.

2
00:00:05.738 --> 00:00:09.263
The cashier asks you to tap again, and this time it works.

3
00:00:09.867 --> 00:00:14.530
So why not let the app do that for you, and try every failed payment again?

4
00:00:14.530 --> 00:00:16.707
Many careful teams do exactly that.

5
00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:18.777
Two questions spoil the plan.

6
00:00:18.777 --> 00:00:21.106
Was the first payment really not made?

7
00:00:21.106 --> 00:00:25.212
And what happens when a thousand apps try again at the same moment?

8
00:00:25.467 --> 00:00:26.920
Start with one payment.

9
00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:29.638
The app sends it, and the bank approves it.

10
00:00:29.638 --> 00:00:32.292
Then the answer gets lost on the way back.

11
00:00:32.533 --> 00:00:34.731
The app waited, and nothing came.

12
00:00:34.731 --> 00:00:35.863
That's a timeout.

13
00:00:35.863 --> 00:00:41.458
So it tries again, and the bank, seeing a perfectly good payment, approves it again.

14
00:00:41.458 --> 00:00:43.056
One coffee, two charges.

15
00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:48.107
And never trying again isn't safe either, because some payments would never go through.

16
00:00:48.107 --> 00:00:51.036
We need to know which failures are safe to try again.

17
00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:53.459
Think of booking a table by phone.

18
00:00:53.459 --> 00:00:55.111
Three things can go wrong.

19
00:00:55.367 --> 00:00:57.282
One, the call never connects.

20
00:00:57.282 --> 00:01:00.585
Nobody heard you, so calling again is always safe.

21
00:01:00.833 --> 00:01:04.797
Two, you say, a table for four at eight, and the line drops.

22
00:01:04.797 --> 00:01:06.317
Did they write it down?

23
00:01:06.317 --> 00:01:07.308
You can't tell.

24
00:01:07.308 --> 00:01:09.884
Calling again might get you two tables.

25
00:01:10.133 --> 00:01:12.661
Three, they answer: sorry, we're full.

26
00:01:12.661 --> 00:01:14.989
Calling again gets the same answer.

27
00:01:15.233 --> 00:01:16.015
Now map it.

28
00:01:16.015 --> 00:01:23.759
A call that never connects is a request that never left: the connection was refused, or connecting timed out.

29
00:01:24.033 --> 00:01:30.391
A dropped line after you spoke is a reset or a read timeout: the request may have arrived.

30
00:01:30.391 --> 00:01:34.770
And sorry, we're full, is an answer, like an error 400 or 500.

31
00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:36.960
The same request gets it again.

32
00:01:37.233 --> 00:01:39.332
The middle case is the hard one.

33
00:01:39.332 --> 00:01:44.382
It's safe only if doing the thing twice has the same effect as doing it once.

34
00:01:44.382 --> 00:01:46.021
That's called idempotent.

35
00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:48.970
Reading a payment's status twice is fine.

36
00:01:48.970 --> 00:01:55.743
Approving a payment twice is not, unless the app sends an idempotency key: a unique id for this payment.

37
00:01:55.967 --> 00:02:01.840
The bank stores the result under it, and a repeat with the same key gets the first result back.

38
00:02:02.133 --> 00:02:04.633
Like giving the restaurant a booking number.

39
00:02:04.633 --> 00:02:08.781
A person might recognise your voice; the bank's program needs the number.

40
00:02:09.267 --> 00:02:11.078
Now the second question.

41
00:02:11.078 --> 00:02:15.077
Picture 1,000 cafés, and the bank's program restarts.

42
00:02:15.077 --> 00:02:18.247
For 250 milliseconds, every payment fails.

43
00:02:18.467 --> 00:02:23.698
After that, the bank can answer 100 requests in each 10 milliseconds.

44
00:02:23.698 --> 00:02:33.402
Every app tries again, and waits longer each time: 100 milliseconds, then 200, 400, 800, at most a second, and six tries in all.

45
00:02:33.402 --> 00:02:35.980
That's called exponential backoff.

46
00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:39.469
How many of the 1,000 cafés get an answer?

47
00:02:39.469 --> 00:02:40.715
Pause and guess.

48
00:02:43.933 --> 00:02:44.578
Only 400.

49
00:02:44.578 --> 00:02:46.081
Look at the arrivals.

50
00:02:46.081 --> 00:02:55.815
Every app works out the same waits, so each round of retries lands in the same 10 milliseconds: a thousand requests where a hundred fit.

51
00:02:56.067 --> 00:03:01.364
A hundred get through, and the rest fail together and come back together.

52
00:03:01.364 --> 00:03:06.299
After six tries, 600 cafés give up, and the bank saw 5,400 requests.

53
00:03:06.533 --> 00:03:13.713
It's like a radio call-in show where everyone who hears the busy tone redials after exactly ten seconds.

54
00:03:13.713 --> 00:03:16.267
The line is jammed again, every time.

55
00:03:16.267 --> 00:03:17.924
Everyone is so punctual.

56
00:03:18.167 --> 00:03:20.004
The fix is to stop agreeing.

57
00:03:20.004 --> 00:03:24.268
Each app waits a random time, between zero and its current limit.

58
00:03:24.268 --> 00:03:25.973
That's called full jitter.

59
00:03:26.233 --> 00:03:28.386
Same bank, same 1,000 cafés.

60
00:03:28.386 --> 00:03:33.536
The retries spread out, never more than 155 in any 10 milliseconds.

61
00:03:33.536 --> 00:03:39.608
Every café gets an answer within about 1.1 seconds, with fewer requests in all.

62
00:03:39.867 --> 00:03:42.053
One more trap: retries multiply.

63
00:03:42.053 --> 00:03:53.875
If a request passes through three programs, each calling the next, and each one tries three times, one request becomes 27 at the bottom, right when the bottom is struggling.

64
00:03:54.100 --> 00:04:02.622
So keep a retry budget: retries may add at most about 10 percent to what you send, and once it's spent, failures go straight back.

65
00:04:02.622 --> 00:04:06.359
And retry at one layer only, the one closest to the user.

66
00:04:06.633 --> 00:04:10.117
So, should the app try every failed payment again?

67
00:04:10.117 --> 00:04:13.391
Only when it's safe: never sent, or idempotent.

68
00:04:13.391 --> 00:04:14.575
At a random time.

69
00:04:14.575 --> 00:04:15.620
From one place.

70
00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:17.331
One quick check.

71
00:04:17.331 --> 00:04:24.114
The app asks the bank for a payment's status, a request that only reads, and the read times out.

72
00:04:24.114 --> 00:04:25.315
Try again or not?

73
00:04:25.315 --> 00:04:26.445
Pause and think.

74
00:04:29.633 --> 00:04:30.371
Try again.

75
00:04:30.371 --> 00:04:34.060
Reading twice changes nothing, so it's idempotent.

76
00:04:34.060 --> 00:04:35.904
Wait a random time first.
